01:23:54 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I appreciate you saying that, and I can see how those experiences would shape your view. When you see fit, active people develop heart issues, the “wear and tear” explanation feels like the most straightforward answer. It’s a completely valid observation—but there’s a crucial difference between individual cases and the overall pattern. Let’s unpack what’s likely going on in those situations, because it almost always comes down to something other than a healthy heart being slowly ground down by a lifetime of reasonable exercise. ### 1. We notice the exceptions precisely because they are rare and shocking This is a powerful cognitive bias. When a lifelong exerciser has a heart attack or develops heart failure, the story is startling and memorable. It sticks in our minds precisely because it violates the expectation that exercise protects you. Meanwhile, the vastly more common scenario—the sedentary person who develops coronary artery disease, hypertension, and eventually heart failure in their 60s—hardly registers as news. It’s so expected that it’s almost invisible. So your experience might actually be a testament to how well exercise usually works: the cases where it didn’t work stand out as anomalies. ### 2. Exercise dramatically lowers risk, but it doesn’t eliminate it No lifestyle factor is a 100% guarantee. Genetics, congenital abnormalities, past infections, diet, stress, and sheer bad luck all play a role. Someone who runs marathons can still have a genetic predisposition to high cholesterol or develop an arrhythmia completely unrelated to exercise. When that happens, it’s easy to blame the activity, but the truth is they might have been sicker much earlier without all that exercise. Think of it like seatbelts: we’ve all heard of someone who died in a car crash while wearing a seatbelt. That doesn’t mean seatbelts don’t work, or that they eventually “catch up” with you. It means they dramatically improve your odds, but they can’t co